INT. MERCANTILE TRUST BANK - SUBLEVEL VAULT CORRIDOR - NIGHT

Concrete. Fluorescent hum. A steel door the size of a garage stands half-open at the end of the hall. A cleaning cart is parked against the wall, its mop bucket empty.

ARTUR VOSS, 40s, in a gray cleaner's coverall, walks down the corridor with a push broom. He moves with a lazy rhythm, humming. LENNOX BAILEY, 30s, in an identical coverall, follows a few steps behind, carrying a clipboard.

ARTUR
You're nervous.

LENNOX
I'm not nervous.

ARTUR
You're humming.

LENNOX
You're humming.

ARTUR
I always hum.

LENNOX
Then so do I.

They stop at the vault door. The broom hangs from Artur's hand. Lennox taps the clipboard on his thigh.

ARTUR
Check the schedule.

LENNOX
It says night staff leaves at midnight.

ARTUR
What time is it?

LENNOX
Eleven fifty-one.

ARTUR
He's late.

LENNOX
He's early. He said eleven fifty-five.

ARTUR
Who said?

LENNOX
He did.

ARTUR
Which he?

LENNOX
(a beat)
The one with the keys.

Artur's smile is thin. He leans the broom against the wall and wipes his palms on his hips.

ARTUR
He told you eleven fifty-five.

LENNOX
He told me.

ARTUR
He told me twelve ten.

LENNOX
Then one of us is lying.

ARTUR
Or he is.

They look at each other. Lennox's clipboard has stopped moving. Down the corridor, an elevator DINGS. Both turn.

LENNOX
That's early.

ARTUR
Someone's coming.

LENNOX
Which one's he?

ARTUR
I don't know.

They stand very still as the elevator doors slide open. A figure steps out, but it is cast in shadow and light, impossible to read. He holds a ring of keys that jingles with each step.

The figure raises one hand. Lennox's hand goes to his coverall pocket. Artur's goes to the mop handle.

LENNOX
(whisper)
Who is it?

ARTUR
(whisper)
Don't move.

The figure stops ten feet away. In the light, a chin, a mouth, a nervous smile.

LENNOX
Mr. Sherwin.

SHERWIN
(off)
Evening, boys.

Artur lets his breath out. Lennox lets his out, too, and both look sheepish.

ARTUR
You're late.

LENNOX
He's early.

The keys jingle. Sherwin steps closer, and in the corridor light we see what the shadows hid: a revolver, hanging loose at his side.

SHERWIN
I'm exactly on time.

Neither man moves.

ARTUR
Mr. Sherwin. What's the gun for?

SHERWIN
For the one of you who's going to ask what the gun is for.

LENNOX
That's Artur.

ARTUR
That's Lennox.

SHERWIN
It doesn't matter.

He raises the gun. Artur dives behind the cart. Lennox hits the floor. The shot rings off concrete and the cart bucket rolls across the corridor, ringing like a bell.

ARTUR
(from behind the cart)
He's got six! I count five!

LENNOX
(from the floor)
Count again!

Another shot. The fluorescent tube above them bursts. Sparks. Darkness. The echo dies in the corridor, and in the dark someone, slowly, begins to hum.
